+### Persistent agent conversations
+
+`server/chats/runtime.js` owns the durable command service, canonical execution host, requests, task delivery, trusted source context and presentation. Ordinary new renderer chats retain their existing preparation, skills/loops/goals and ownership path until a server command manages them; legacy ownership drains and flushes before takeover. A provider-native child marked `origin: native` stays inspect-only. `src/chat/managed-chat-bridge.ts` observes managed chats through the existing transcript/painter; it does not run a second engine or write observer history back through session PATCH.
+
+Chat, assignment run, execution, attempt, logical message and command receipt are separate stable identities. Spawn commits child/profile/command/outbox together; the existing sub-agent graph remains the sole family scheduler. A reservation obtains exact FIFO ownership, then inference activates only after durable `attempt.started`. Terminal execution persistence precedes the journal end, immutable task result and parent command admission; `result.delivered` acknowledges an accepted recipient command, not a transient SSE listener. Later child-local sends use local-only completion and cannot rewrite an earlier task result or wake its original parent. Inline waits use durable hold/ack disposition to avoid a second wake. Epoch/disposal checks fence old deliveries and late spawns.
+
+Managed input is auto/queue/steer: auto starts idle or queues busy, queue preserves an ordered next turn, steer requires an active capable owner and is consumed at a shared tool-round boundary with canonical row IDs. Ordered inputs, edits/removal/push and retry receipts retain their captured provider/model/options/profile. Per-chat revision controls reject stale queue edits. Stale ownership and unknown effects never gain authority from TTL expiry. `turn` stops only the selected reply and holds earlier unapplied input; human-only `agents` preserves the parent reply while cancelling captured descendants and disposing old wake authority; `tree` includes the parent. Legacy Stop is pinned to its original renderer owner, so delayed requests cannot stop a replacement turn.
+
+`server/chats/model-selection.js` resolves explicit complete pair → agent type pair → global sub-agent routing pair → parent selection, validates advertised reasoning/thinking options, and preserves explicit errors. A selected pool remains a pool and reports the actual resolved model separately; loadable local models retain their identity while awaiting load/capacity. Child model configuration changes future input only and never updates the ordinary new-chat remembered model. Prompt/profile snapshots are immutable evidence; every admission and effect intersects current policy and freshly verified project/checkout scope. Model choice is not permission escalation.
+
+`agent_capabilities`, `chat_list`, `chat_read`, `chat_send`, `chat_wait` and `chat_stop` are one shared tool facade for native and authenticated renderer/CLI execution. Callers are branded server objects derived from a live fenced execution; public JSON cannot supply actor, source scope, approval proof or causal budgets. Reads are bounded and same-project; sends cannot target broader authority. `chat_wait` pins one execution, returns blocked/terminal/timeout honestly, and does not cancel on timeout. Durable per-human-root budgets, hop/inbox limits and live wait-cycle guards prevent endless peer exchanges; consumed human steering establishes a fresh root, consumed peer steering retains its lineage. Public `chat_stop` exposes turn/tree; human UI also exposes agents scope.
+
+`server/chats/requests.js` persists independent per-chat/per-execution question and invocation-specific approval records with immutable answer receipts. Questions can reattach the original execution/attempt across restart only at an exact canonical unanswered ask boundary, after quiescence proof, without unresolved tool intents. The host appends the saved tool answer once before inference. Approvals without their live callback expire; unrecoverable/terminal orphan questions expire without losing saved bodies. Raw bound renderer approvals use private active-owner proof without manufacturing a managed marker. Host capabilities are project-scoped, invocation-pinned and require revalidation immediately before an effect; uncertain dispatch retains a recovery barrier.
+
+The application dependency factory is `server/runtime/server-runner-deps.js`; pure `server/runner/` stays free of application graph/configuration imports. The shared preparation bundle is built for source startup and packaged execution. `server/usage/ledger.js` supplies the same ledger arithmetic to renderer and managed execution; one canonical `chat_usage_receipts` row per execution/round commits child-owned usage without parent double-counting. Actual model and last-request context usage are projected separately from pool identity and CLI aggregate accounting.
+
+Protected summary projections carry relationship, latest execution, exact pending command, request IDs/kinds, task obligations and read cursors without transcript/profile bodies. Token updates use bounded coalesced live-only frames; collapsed children do not receive per-token notices or trigger transcript/sidebar rebuilds. Incoming peer/result rows keep typed provenance out of provider input. Managed acknowledgement uses the exact visible message/terminal-execution cursor and changes only read/error flags; an old running cursor cannot pre-acknowledge a later failure. Requests, queue holds, capacity/host waits and recovery remain distinct from generating spinners and completion notifications.
+
+`/api/chats/capabilities` advertises minimum reader/writer protocol `1`; new endpoint writes and host connections require version `1`. Paired devices can submit scoped human commands and answers, not host or agent authority. The existing-ID CLI uses the server service, including previously ordinary saved chats after legacy drain; explicit conflicting overrides, disabled creation for an unmanaged target and incompatible/unreachable servers fail without local fallback. Only a specific absent-chat response permits fresh CLI persistence. Ephemeral local CLI runs do not gain conversation-tool authority. Disabling new creation retains recovery and human continuation of saved managed chats; an old binary does not understand these markers and requires the pre-upgrade backup procedure.
+
+The normal transcript/composer renders managed children, grouped delegation cards, source-chat messages, inherited ceilings and model controls. Past-history mutation helpers explicitly reject managed/imported/native transcripts: no edit/regenerate/undo/truncate/fork/branch rewind in v1; queue edits, copy/export and file review remain. Legacy inspectors retain their data; human Continue as chat creates a marked partial archival context with no execution replay or parent auto-wake. Deleting a parent is blocked by active/queued/request work in the family; settled children and checkout files are retained.
+
+**Managed metadata concurrency:** The protected conversation state keeps a separate presentationRevision counter for actual name, groupId, pinnedAt and composerDraft changes, including authoritative rename/pin configuration. GET/full summaries expose chatPresentationRevisions; new PUT/PATCH writers supply captured chatBasePresentationRevisions. Canonical output and acknowledgement revisions do not invalidate that metadata fence. Omitted managed bases fall back to strict canonical checks; unmanaged entries are rejected, and deletion always retains its canonical fence. Only a fully validated managed-only metadata PATCH without scalar/group/delete work can bypass a stale global revision; mixed writes keep global rebase and must retain their original presentation bases. Exact committed counters are captured inside the transaction and echoed on PUT/PATCH. Full frames pair agentPresentationRevision with the complete shell snapshot; partial notices and dirty local edits cannot silently rebase it. No-op writes do not advance it, and rollback covers both fields and counter. See the contributor recovery contract for compatibility details.
